    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One thing the team is worried about is the \"unpredictability\" factor. Even with a roundabout, we see these sudden, erratic movements—drivers braking hard for no reason or cutting people off—that seem to disrupt the flow and cause these little pockets of chaos. From an engineering perspective, how would you quantify that kind of irregular friction or \"jitter\" in the traffic stream, and is there a way to model it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This erratic behavior, or \"jitter,\" can be quantified using metrics like traffic variability or turbulence in traffic flow data. Engineers often model this using microscopic simulation tools, such as VISSIM, which allows for inputting driver behavioral parameters. These simulations can then visualize and measure how abrupt braking and lane changes translate into shockwaves and disruptions within the overall flow.",
      "source": "target",
